{"id":"vehicle_exhaust","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::vehicle_exhaust","source_path":"vehicle_exhaust/vehicle_exhaust.json","category":"Smoke","author":"Autodesk","description":"An aero simulation graph producing a racing vehicle exhaust effect.  ","documentation_file":"vehicle_exhaust_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Vehicle Exhaust\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses aero to create a vehicle exhaust smoke simulation\n\n### Inputs and outputs \n\nInputs for this graph are the meshes you wish to emit smoke from. The output is the resulting aero volume from the smoke simulation.  \n\n### Useful information\n\n- This graph relies heavily on varying multiple `source_air` properties (using the `vary_source_property` node) to produce the chaotic and dynamic motion required for this effect. Play with the `multiplier` on the `vary_speed` and `vary_inherit_velocity` to scale the spread and pushback respectively. The solver `Style` is also set to `fluffy` (cubic transport interpolation) on the `aero_solver_settings`, to produce the billowy look. Finally some turbulence is applied to add some randomness.\n\n- Performance is directly related to the `Fluid Detail Size` on the `source_air` node, and the length of the trail which is controlled by the `dissipation_influence`. Decrease the `Fluid Detail Size` and increase the `Max Steps` on the `aero_solver_settings` for more detail. A `vorticity_influence` may be used as well.  \n\n- The `Scene Units In Meters` is set to 0.1 to better approximate smoke behavior at a real life scale. Please adjust accordingly.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"cube_mesh","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"sphere_mesh","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"aero_volume","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::term